## Getting started

```sh
just dev-setup       # one-time bootstrap (nix shell + pre-commit hooks)
nix develop          # or `direnv allow` for automatic activation
```

Authenticate against {{ cloud | upper }}:

{% if cloud == 'gcp' -%}
```sh
gcloud auth login
gcloud auth application-default login
```
{%- elif cloud == 'aws' -%}
```sh
aws sso login    # or configure credentials for your profile
```
{%- elif cloud == 'azure' -%}
```sh
az login
```
{%- endif %}

## Layout

```
terraform/
├── main.tf            # root module — keep thin, split into modules/ as it grows
├── variables.tf
├── outputs.tf
├── versions.tf        # providers + remote state backend
└── environments/      # per-environment backend + variable files
    ├── dev/
    ├── test/
    └── prod/
```

## Workflow

```sh
just plan dev        # init + plan against dev
just apply dev       # apply the saved plan
just lint            # fmt-check, tflint, validate
just scan            # trivy security scan
just docs            # regenerate terraform-docs
just ci              # what CI runs
```

State lives in remote backends configured per environment under
`terraform/environments/<env>/backend.hcl`. Create the state
{{ 'bucket' if cloud != 'azure' else 'storage account' }} once per environment before
the first `just init <env>`.

Commit `.terraform.lock.hcl` after the first `init`. It pins provider selections
and signed checksums; state files and saved plans remain ignored and must never be
committed.

## Conventions

- One state per environment; never share state across environments.
- Every resource carries the `common_tags` / labels from `main.tf`.
- No secrets in `.tfvars` — feed secrets through environment variables
  (`TF_VAR_...`) or your secret manager.
- In CI, use short-lived OIDC workload identity, review the saved plan, pin actions
  to commit SHAs, and apply that reviewed plan rather than planning again.
